<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thirumangai Alvar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th–9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Tirumangai Alvar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thirumangai Alvar was a Tamil poet-saint devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, traditionally the last of the twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a>, who lived in the eighth or ninth century. Said to have been a local chieftain before his conversion, he visited many Vishnu temples and contributed more hymns than any other Alvar to the Nalayira Divya Prabandham, including verses on the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamallapuram">Mamallapuram</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thirumangai-alvar.jpg" alt="Thirumangai Alvar" width="320" height="621" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chronikhiles · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tirumangai_Alvar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tradition places him in year 399 of the Kali Yuga, 2702 or 2706 BCE; modern scholars date the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a> between the 5th and 9th centuries, though their dates remain disputed. He is generally held to have been a contemporary of the Pallava king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nandivarman-ii">Nandivarman II</a>, since his hymns refer to the later Pallavas. His life is told in hagiographies of the 11th to 14th centuries. Born Kaliyan in Thirukuraiyalur near Sirkazhi, a son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> general Nilam, he was a skilled archer and served the Chola king as a commander, receiving the title Parakala and the small territory of Ali Nadu, whose capital Thirumangai gave him the name Thirumangai Mannan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to tradition he fell in love with Kumudavalli, adopted daughter of a Vaishnava doctor, who required him to become a Vaishnava and feed a thousand people daily for a year. Unable to bear the cost, he turned to highway robbery, and while robbing a wedding party he found that the bridegroom was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> himself, who taught him the eight-syllable mantra. The first ten verses of his Periya Tirumoli tell of this change. Giving up his chieftainship, he visited 88 of the 108 Divya Desams, spread the poems of earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a> and preached devotion rather than penance; a later account tells of a meeting with the Shaiva saint Thirugnana Sambandhar at Sirkazhi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His six Tamil works, 1,361 verses in all, form the largest share of any Alvar in the Divya Prabandham, and Tamil Vaishnavas regard them as the six Angas to the four poems of Nammalvar, held as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>. The chief work, the Periya Tirumoli of 1,084 hymns, praises over 40 forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badrinath">Badrinath</a> to Thirukkurungudi. In Tirunetuntantakam he speaks as a woman parted from her divine beloved, and in the two Tirumatal poems, as a gopika, he threatens <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> with the matal, the rejected lover's ancient Tamil custom.</p><p>He holds the titles Narkavi Perumal, the excellent poet, and Parakala, and Vaishnavas consider him an incarnation of Sharanga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>'s bow. A temple was dedicated to him in the Vitthala complex at Hampi. Each year in the month of Thai his image is carried to the eleven Thirunaangur temples, where his verses for each are chanted. K. C. Varadachari called him a rebel, a social reformer, even a kind of Robin Hood, and above all an exquisite lyricist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thirumangai Alvar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Pallava age saw the decisive rise of devotional Hinduism in the Tamil country: the Shaiva Nayanars, such as Appar and Sambandar, and the Vaishnava Alvars, such as <b>Tirumangai Alvar</b>, composed Tamil hymns that drew worshippers to temples and turned many away from Jainism and Buddhism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
